the repository, for npm provenance (#23)\n\nnpm refused the 1.0.3 publish: a provenance statement is checked against\npackage.json''s repository.url, which was empty. Every published package\nnames https://github.com/datalayer/reactor, the plugins with their directory.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtyghCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAMgMQACSJ0Hu/g9lOP28JcrGqug48\nQNle0r6uoNp6NPpNXA1fkIdXBfzkg8J1w5c8/BxcXxeb791gk6+vpQhM2RLbrVxR\nKDIgzAPuPRKdo/56eBqfB7e0Wn0KQNYzT67LaVMOAqn+Qwg95fx9VXm+rtc62woV\nLpAwEuA4xTwBJ950fnvayiyBJjCh3ubzlwaNxZlbijdEh8r78QkicyxnIgGiCpff\nxmzluuMcPy1+S8/6FH3r9wzFbWTjMKKc6eP5tMr+70tFrf54SLq6RoUYUeYNMRz1\nNWhHpxOQW0QCMX7X6PemfirQX3YRbwzuxYakg3t/wMn98VxtrksBVDY8Qo9ZUbw8\nDVtOH0BEw6LqmPz+kRrn64TcM6B9RE3aTqsIhzpTNmWnQo/R8fMOIDoMT4x8iLO4\n4R6ipwbarO7j/syJFpfDYGBgJMlvvpMnFv4iNs7fMl2q4elxCYYlls0ox8cv3Vr0\neOM8jiJBzv0nBeO7b9VYAdTmDzbFE0B8+OSes/IH2q6Vn45f/wveLtXPH4udQxJW\nczVABT2iCFpHUkY2O47G14zn9tWKXYkQ5DT4o2btPxiNgFY4FZ+i3bPTzLyNMvDZ\nBVUk+0TfQzZ3pMgUljXWVAn65vcmgYQWpE0rMaBwySLLub1Dwjvfk0CL50AdSOuU\nBMnX9oYf6s5vjjpAiPaL\n=f/gO\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-26T02:04:17Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/datalayer/reactor/commit/a8b2e48185445f70c00a7d6a353be44882b8f60d","node_id":"C_kwDOR_wP0doAKGE4YjJlNDgxODU0NDVmNzBjMDBhN2Q2YTM1M2JlNDQ4ODJiOGY2MGQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/datalayer/reactor/commit/07cc66995492d08f4f1b466e9fd27f8e0f2bed17","sha":"07cc66995492d08f4f1b466e9fd27f8e0f2bed17","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/datalayer/reactor/commits/07cc66995492d08f4f1b466e9fd27f8e0f2bed17"}],"sha":"a8b2e48185445f70c00a7d6a353be44882b8f60d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/datalayer/reactor/commits/a8b2e48185445f70c00a7d6a353be44882b8f60d"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/226720?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/echarles","id":226720,"login":"echarles","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjIyNjcyMA==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/datalayer/reactor/commits/07cc66995492d08f4f1b466e9fd27f8e0f2bed17/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-26T01:58:07Z","email":"226720+echarles@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Eric Charles"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-26T01:58:07Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Depend on the released Datalayer packages (#22)\n\n* A contribution can be extended\n\nA plugin offers something — a view, a command, a tool — and another plugin\nwants to add to *that* one rather than offer a rival. Its only lever was to\ncontribute a replacement and hope to be read last, which makes the outcome\ndepend on the order plugins happened to load in: it works on the machine\nwhere the names sort the right way and nowhere else.\n\nAn extension is an ordinary contribution to a derived point, so order,\nidentity, tenant filtering and disposal with the plugin all hold for\nextensions too, and a host reads them without a second mechanism.\n\n* reactor_mcp_server 1.0.0: an extensible MCP server\n\nThree ideas. A tool is a contribution, so what a server is made of can be\nread before one is built. A contribution can be extended, so an extension\nnarrows somebody else''s tool by name and in a declared order instead of\nregistering a rival and hoping to load second. And the URL decides what is\nserved: tools belong to named toolsets, a client asks for them with\n`/mcp?benchmarks`, and an extension nobody asked for is not registered at\nall — reactor holds it on an activation event until a URL names its toolset.\n\nTested against a real MCP client over HTTP, which is where the two things a\ndesign like this gets wrong turned up: a mounted sub-application answers a\nredirect the protocol does not follow, and one whose lifespan nobody runs\nanswers ''Task group is not initialized'' to everything.\n\n* reactor_mcp_server: an extension may act on the built server\n\nThe escape hatch the Datalayer gateway needs, and deliberately a narrow one:\neverything an extension offers goes through the contribution points, where a\nhost can read it without running anything, and `on_server` is for what an\nextension has to do *to* a server — take off a tool a scaffold registered\noutside the contribution model, add what the SDK exposes and a spec does not\ndescribe.\n\nCalled after the tools are on, per built server, so an extension whose\ntoolset is not active is not called; one that raises costs only itself.\n\n* reactor_mcp_server 1.0.1: what a deployment decides\n\nThree seams a host that serves over HTTP needs, found by putting the Datalayer\nMCP gateway on this.\n\n**The server a selection is served by is the deployment''s to make.**\n`McpHost.build` made a bare `MCPServer`; the gateway''s carries CORS, an\nidentity middleware and the token verifier the operator configured, and a\nclient reaching a toolset through a bare one would reach it unauthenticated.\n`server_factory` is how a host says what to build.\n\n**So are the transport options.** `stateless_http`, the body limit, the\ntransport settings — the SDK takes them where the application is built, which\nthe router does. A deployment that needs sessions was getting the SDK''s\ndefault instead, and a client waiting for an `Mcp-Session-Id` was handed\nnothing. `app_options` are passed to every selection''s application: another\ntoolset is not another transport.\n\n**A lifespan gets a task of its own.** It was opened inline in the request\nthat first asked for a set of toolsets and closed on shutdown, which is a\ncancel scope entered in one task and left in another: anyio raises at the end\n— \"Attempted to exit cancel scope in a different task than it was entered in\"\n— so a worker''s last words were \"Application shutdown failed\", after the work\nwas done and where nobody is looking.\n\nAnd, from the same migration: a tool that names no toolset goes in its\nextension''s **first declared** one rather than one named after the plugin. An\nextension declaring `sandboxes` under the name `sandboxes-datalayer` put its\ntools in a toolset nobody declared, and a toolset nobody declared is never\nactive — 35 tools contributed, 14 served, nothing saying why.\n\n* reactor_mcp_server 1.0.2: a toolset name is a subject, not a plugin\n\nTwo extensions adding to the same toolset declare the same name — which is\nhow a deployment puts its own sandbox tools in `sandboxes` beside the ones\nthat ship, and how `/toolsets` came to list `sandboxes` twice, with two\ndescriptions, to a client asking what there is.\n\nThe name identifies it. The first declaration is the one described; the rest\nadd their tools and say so at debug.\n\n* Document the MCP server foundation\n\nSix pages under a section of its own, because `reactor_mcp_server` is the one\nplugin package that is also a product: a deployment installs a distribution\nand its tools are served, which is worth explaining as its own subject rather\nthan as a paragraph in the Python runtime.\n\n- **Tools** — offering one, what a `ToolSpec` carries, where the description\n  comes from, and putting them on a server by hand.\n- **Extending a tool** — what replaced registering the same name and hoping to\n  load second, what a `ToolExtension` may change, the order they compose in,\n  and why extending a tool nobody offered is not an error.\n- **Toolsets** — the URL grammar, `always` and `default`, activation events,\n  and that a toolset name is a subject rather than a package.\n- **The host** — building a server, what a `BuiltServer` carries, the server\n  factory and `on_server`, and what one misbehaving plugin costs.\n- **Serving** — the three routes, running the installed extensions, transport\n  options, and the two things a host of the SDK''s app must get right.\n- **Writing an extension** — class to published distribution, with the\n  checklist.\n\nEvery claim about the API is checked against the code, and each example is\nrun rather than written from memory. Six mermaid diagrams, all of which parse\nand draw; the site builds with no broken links.\n\n* toolsets\n\n* Depend on the released Datalayer packages\n\nVersion floors only: @datalayer/primer-addons 1.0.23, @datalayer/core 1.2.12, @datalayer/agent-runtimes 1.3.13, @datalayer/jupyter-react, jupyter-lexical, jupyter-embed and jupyter-docusaurus-plugin 2.0.17; no code change.\n\n* CI installs the MCP server plugin, whose tests run with the rest\n\n* CI: the MCP plugin''s async tests run under asyncio_mode auto with its test extra\n\n* 1.0.3: one version for every package; release.yaml publishes them all on a tag, trusted publishing\n\n- @datalayer/reactor, reactor-commands, reactor-graph, reactor-manager and\n  reactor-shell at 1.0.3, the plugins'' floor on @datalayer/reactor with it;\n  datalayer-reactor follows package.json; reactor-mcp-server 1.0.3 with its\n  floor on datalayer_reactor raised.\n- release.yaml (was release.yml): on a v* tag, checks the tag names every\n  package''s version, builds and tests, packs and publishes the five npm\n  packages (root first) through the npm environment and the two Python\n  packages through the pypi environment — OIDC, no token — skipping what\n  is already on the registries; then a GitHub release.\n- ci.yml and fix-license-header.yml renamed to .yaml.\n- RELEASE.md: the packages, the trusted publisher settings, the steps.\n\n* RELEASE.md formatted\n\n* plugins keep their floor on a published @datalayer/reactor\n\nThe root package is not a workspace member, so npm resolves it from the\nregistry: a floor on the version being released cannot install (ETARGET).\n\n* MCP server: what the review found\n\n- The router follows the host''s revision: an extension added or the cache\n  dropped means new applications for new connections, not the stale ones.\n- on_reactor_start / on_reactor_stop reach on_start / on_stop (pluggy\n  hookimpl); the platform never called the latter.\n- Tools without a declared toolset get one declared, named after the\n  extension, so they can be selected at all.\n- A tenant is a dimension of every read and of the cache key; the router\n  takes a tenant resolver.\n- Resources, prompts and on_server belong to the selection like tools do:\n  an extension that is not participating puts nothing on the server.\n- A held extension''s toolsets are declared before anybody asks for them, so\n  /toolsets can name them.\n- A wrapper keeps the tool''s docstring description; the manifest declares\n  the resources and prompts points; __version__ is the distribution''s.\n- reactor core: reading a contribution''s extensions wakes a plugin held on\n  the derived point.\n- cms-astro example: jupyter-lexical ^2.0.17.\nTests for each, in tests/test_review_follow_ups.py.","tree":{"sha":"1b7373b1071b5024267823a4fd0d9c279b973819","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/datalayer/reactor/git/trees/1b7373b1071b5024267823a4fd0d9c279b973819"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/datalayer/reactor/git/commits/07cc66995492d08f4f1b466e9fd27f8e0f2bed17","verification":{"payload":"tree 1b7373b1071b5024267823a4fd0d9c279b973819\nparent db81a9f35f87de3a5eabd97ae9ac76adc12d87c0\nauthor Eric Charles <226720+echarles@users.noreply.github.com> 1790387887 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790387887 +0200\n\nDepend on the released Datalayer packages (#22)\n\n* A contribution can be extended\n\nA plugin offers something — a view, a command, a tool — and another plugin\nwants to add to *that* one rather than offer a rival. Its only lever was to\ncontribute a replacement and hope to be read last, which makes the outcome\ndepend on the order plugins happened to load in: it works on the machine\nwhere the names sort the right way and nowhere else.\n\nAn extension is an ordinary contribution to a derived point, so order,\nidentity, tenant filtering and disposal with the plugin all hold for\nextensions too, and a host reads them without a second mechanism.\n\n* reactor_mcp_server 1.0.0: an extensible MCP server\n\nThree ideas. A tool is a contribution, so what a server is made of can be\nread before one is built. A contribution can be extended, so an extension\nnarrows somebody else''s tool by name and in a declared order instead of\nregistering a rival and hoping to load second. And the URL decides what is\nserved: tools belong to named toolsets, a client asks for them with\n`/mcp?benchmarks`, and an extension nobody asked for is not registered at\nall — reactor holds it on an activation event until a URL names its toolset.\n\nTested against a real MCP client over HTTP, which is where the two things a\ndesign like this gets wrong turned up: a mounted sub-application answers a\nredirect the protocol does not follow, and one whose lifespan nobody runs\nanswers ''Task group is not initialized'' to everything.\n\n* reactor_mcp_server: an extension may act on the built server\n\nThe escape hatch the Datalayer gateway needs, and deliberately a narrow one:\neverything an extension offers goes through the contribution points, where a\nhost can read it without running anything, and `on_server` is for what an\nextension has to do *to* a server — take off a tool a scaffold registered\noutside the contribution model, add what the SDK exposes and a spec does not\ndescribe.\n\nCalled after the tools are on, per built server, so an extension whose\ntoolset is not active is not called; one that raises costs only itself.\n\n* reactor_mcp_server 1.0.1: what a deployment decides\n\nThree seams a host that serves over HTTP needs, found by putting the Datalayer\nMCP gateway on this.\n\n**The server a selection is served by is the deployment''s to make.**\n`McpHost.build` made a bare `MCPServer`; the gateway''s carries CORS, an\nidentity middleware and the token verifier the operator configured, and a\nclient reaching a toolset through a bare one would reach it unauthenticated.\n`server_factory` is how a host says what to build.\n\n**So are the transport options.** `stateless_http`, the body limit, the\ntransport settings — the SDK takes them where the application is built, which\nthe router does. A deployment that needs sessions was getting the SDK''s\ndefault instead, and a client waiting for an `Mcp-Session-Id` was handed\nnothing. `app_options` are passed to every selection''s application: another\ntoolset is not another transport.\n\n**A lifespan gets a task of its own.** It was opened inline in the request\nthat first asked for a set of toolsets and closed on shutdown, which is a\ncancel scope entered in one task and left in another: anyio raises at the end\n— \"Attempted to exit cancel scope in a different task than it was entered in\"\n— so a worker''s last words were \"Application shutdown failed\", after the work\nwas done and where nobody is looking.\n\nAnd, from the same migration: a tool that names no toolset goes in its\nextension''s **first declared** one rather than one named after the plugin. An\nextension declaring `sandboxes` under the name `sandboxes-datalayer` put its\ntools in a toolset nobody declared, and a toolset nobody declared is never\nactive — 35 tools contributed, 14 served, nothing saying why.\n\n* reactor_mcp_server 1.0.2: a toolset name is a subject, not a plugin\n\nTwo extensions adding to the same toolset declare the same name — which is\nhow a deployment puts its own sandbox tools in `sandboxes` beside the ones\nthat ship, and how `/toolsets` came to list `sandboxes` twice, with two\ndescriptions, to a client asking what there is.\n\nThe name identifies it. The first declaration is the one described; the rest\nadd their tools and say so at debug.\n\n* Document the MCP server foundation\n\nSix pages under a section of its own, because `reactor_mcp_server` is the one\nplugin package that is also a product: a deployment installs a distribution\nand its tools are served, which is worth explaining as its own subject rather\nthan as a paragraph in the Python runtime.\n\n- **Tools** — offering one, what a `ToolSpec` carries, where the description\n  comes from, and putting them on a server by hand.\n- **Extending a tool** — what replaced registering the same name and hoping to\n  load second, what a `ToolExtension` may change, the order they compose in,\n  and why extending a tool nobody offered is not an error.\n- **Toolsets** — the URL grammar, `always` and `default`, activation events,\n  and that a toolset name is a subject rather than a package.\n- **The host** — building a server, what a `BuiltServer` carries, the server\n  factory and `on_server`, and what one misbehaving plugin costs.\n- **Serving** — the three routes, running the installed extensions, transport\n  options, and the two things a host of the SDK''s app must get right.\n- **Writing an extension** — class to published distribution, with the\n  checklist.\n\nEvery claim about the API is checked against the code, and each example is\nrun rather than written from memory. Six mermaid diagrams, all of which parse\nand draw; the site builds with no broken links.\n\n* toolsets\n\n* Depend on the released Datalayer packages\n\nVersion floors only: @datalayer/primer-addons 1.0.23, @datalayer/core 1.2.12, @datalayer/agent-runtimes 1.3.13, @datalayer/jupyter-react, jupyter-lexical, jupyter-embed and jupyter-docusaurus-plugin 2.0.17; no code change.\n\n* CI installs the MCP server plugin, whose tests run with the rest\n\n* CI: the MCP plugin''s async tests run under asyncio_mode auto with its test extra\n\n* 1.0.3: one version for every package; release.yaml publishes them all on a tag, trusted publishing\n\n- @datalayer/reactor, reactor-commands, reactor-graph, reactor-manager and\n  reactor-shell at 1.0.3, the plugins'' floor on @datalayer/reactor with it;\n  datalayer-reactor follows package.json; reactor-mcp-server 1.0.3 with its\n  floor on datalayer_reactor raised.\n- release.yaml (was release.yml): on a v* tag, checks the tag names every\n  package''s version, builds and tests, packs and publishes the five npm\n  packages (root first) through the npm environment and the two Python\n  packages through the pypi environment — OIDC, no token — skipping what\n  is already on the registries; then a GitHub release.\n- ci.yml and fix-license-header.yml renamed to .yaml.\n- RELEASE.md: the packages, the trusted publisher settings, the steps.\n\n* RELEASE.md formatted\n\n* plugins keep their floor on a published @datalayer/reactor\n\nThe root package is not a workspace member, so npm resolves it from the\nregistry: a floor on the version being released cannot install (ETARGET).\n\n* MCP server: what the review found\n\n- The router follows the host''s revision: an extension added or the cache\n  dropped means new applications for new connections, not the stale ones.\n- on_reactor_start / on_reactor_stop reach on_start / on_stop (pluggy\n  hookimpl); the platform never called the latter.\n- Tools without a declared toolset get one declared, named after the\n  extension, so they can be selected at all.\n- A tenant is a dimension of every read and of the cache key; the router\n  takes a tenant resolver.\n- Resources, prompts and on_server belong to the selection like tools do:\n  an extension that is not participating puts nothing on the server.\n- A held extension''s toolsets are declared before anybody asks for them, so\n  /toolsets can name them.\n- A wrapper keeps the tool''s docstring description; the manifest declares\n  the resources and prompts points; __version__ is the distribution''s.\n- reactor core: reading a contribution''s extensions wakes a plugin held on\n  the derived point.\n- cms-astro example: jupyter-lexical ^2.0.17.\nTests for each, in tests/test_review_follow_ups.py.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtyavCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAmXsQAFt4xyxRD0vWE7Exsh+zZpBe\nvZxjCfV25s9Drri+lyjxljGJn9Rns5M0Wy3PagDniahnbrhjIof/B6/4xuSH7DTC\nJmC87sw/o07pSvokSDm/4DsGHou7OcLAU4CW7XCh2V6HObF43XnZwvH8G94FZBY3\n4fPqlegP+ZQ7ojoQw1pHwXt+GCNzm2SDxB/LKyViUIL/MWiRzHwIutfparVEiydR\nrHHhKbGI8au2qH4mRAPOETzOYB8I1oHB/Sv/oTG0gwX9gylqZKIcJX4Qa4CPyObN\nrst5IzW1AVbq1hYbj7qsQekXD42tad8769VERJ1TuoLkPfi5lnxmulCRSVypFuCO\nlZF7JqijvZ7RJ0LlTkxL2BjlF+9LPGnD51sYYOKLRskAZO1vBE6xwzNdnz8zc3To\nWa1CAamJbEX2u9OjlOarHTq2rl0E/8iyc1++ADhC0mHyOWKRGwRKCY+CE2NbH5fr\na8PrzNeZE5Of0O+mQITokNmPmRpxFouWMHn1Y42RWZ/em