origin — no npm afterwards, no second server,\nno CORS.\n\n- `reactor.host`: `create_reactor_host` is `create_reactor_app` plus the two\n  things every backend was writing by hand — serve a built single-page UI, and\n  discover extensions on boot. Named *host* rather than *shell*: the shell here\n  is already the browser-side container, and the docs have used \"host\" for the\n  application that runs plugins throughout.\n\n- Route order is the design, and the tests are about the two ways it goes\n  wrong. A client-side route must survive a refresh (`/graph` → the app), and\n  an API path must never be answered with HTML — a catch-all otherwise\n  swallows the *near* misses too, and a client expecting JSON fails parsing\n  HTML with no idea why. The prefix scan recurses, because FastAPI 0.141 does\n  not flatten an included router into `app.routes`.\n\n- `examples/music/backend` becomes `datalayer_music_example`: depends on the\n  four plugin packages, ships the built interface in its wheel under `share/`,\n  exposes the console script. The plugins stay separate distributions, because\n  a plugin being its own installable is the lesson.\n\n- The interface asks its own origin. The hard-coded `http://localhost:8799`\n  was the quiet reason the example needed two servers; development injects the\n  URL, production does not.\n\n- `catalog-core` (phase 7.1): the data contract with no design system in it.\n  Every plugin wanting the songs used to import Primer whether it drew with it\n  or not — invisible until a store uses a different kit.\n\n127 TS tests, 73 Python tests, four examples and the docs site all build.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: point the extension walkthrough at the console script\n\nThe ''install while it runs'' demonstration reads better against the command a\nperson would actually have running, now that one exists.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat: a CMS example — two Python packages, one set of contribution points\n\nM5 of REACTOR.md, and the sharpest statement of the model so far:\n\n    Python package → Extension → Plugin → Contribution → Contribution point\n    cms              Core        Gallery  a content type  cms.contentType\n    cms-pro          Pro         Product  a content type  cms.contentType\n\n`make cms && datalayer-cms` serves a shadcn/ui CMS whose every feature arrived\nfrom a Python package. Then `pip install examples/cms/cms-pro` **while it is\nrunning**, refresh, and three more plugins appear in the same three points.\n\nThe point is what is *absent*. There is no plugin API for paid plugins, no\ncapability flag, no tier check. `cms-pro` advertises itself under the same\nentry-point group, registers on the same platform, fills the same point ids —\npackaging and licensing sit at the top of that chain, the extension mechanism\nsits at the bottom, and nothing between them knows which package was paid for.\n\nThree points, three shapes, so \"contribution point\" is not read as \"list of\nbuttons\": the toolbar draws every contribution, content types draw one, and the\npublish lifecycle runs them and lets any one veto. The SEO validator refuses a\npublish; the social publisher only announces one.\n\nThis also closes #11 by a different route than \"port the music store\". What\nneeded proving is that a plugin need not know the host''s UI kit — shown twice:\nmost contributions are plain records, and the one plugin that draws borrows the\nhost''s shadcn components through setReactorSharedModules and inherits its theme.\n\nFour fixes the example forced, each a real defect:\n\n- `bootstrapExtensions` flattened extensions, losing the grouping the server\n  knew. It now returns `defineExtension` groups, so \"what would I uninstall to\n  lose this?\" survives the wire.\n- A failed entry point was remembered as hopeless. The commonest failure is an\n  entry point advertised before its module is importable, so it is retried and\n  only the log is de-duplicated.\n- `share/` was looked for beside the package only — which works until somebody\n  installs the wheel, where data files land under `sys.prefix`. `find_share`\n  looks in both.\n- The discovery tests scanned the real entry-point group, so `pip install`\n  anything and the suite changed its mind. The group is now a platform property\n  and the tests use their own.\n\n128 TS tests, 74 Python tests, five examples and the docs site all build.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: review findings, a base `reactor` server, and `make all`\n\nSix defects from review of this branch, each with a test:\n\n- `unregister_plugin` bumped the revision before the lookup, so asking about a\n  plugin that does not exist woke every SSE client to say nothing happened.\n- `assertAllowed` treated `//evil.example/x.js` as a local path. It is\n  protocol-relative, and a browser loads it cross-origin — the check now\n  resolves the URL against the page rather than pattern-matching it. This one\n  was a hole, not a wrinkle.\n- `useBackendPluginStream` built `${backendUrl}/...`, so a trailing slash gave\n  `//plugins/state`: one URL in two forms, in every log and cache key.\n- `setBackendPlugins` never cleared its revive list when a plugin was stood\n  down for some other reason, so a server returning could undo a deactivation\n  it had nothing to do with. An explicit `deactivate` now claims the plugin,\n  before the early return — deactivating something already down changes no\n  state but does express intent.\n- `rescan_extensions` remembering a failed load as hopeless was already fixed\n  in 148b271; a test now pins it.\n- `cms` and `datalayer_music_example` wheels did not build: setuptools''\n  `data-files` copies files, not directories, and a built interface is\n  `index.html` beside `static/`. Both are on hatchling''s `shared-data`.\n\nThat last one is what `make all` found. A target that builds and installs\neverything is a test of the packaging no unit test was going to be.\n\nNew:\n\n- `reactor` on the command line — a server with no plugins of its own. It has a\n  platform, the management API and a scan of the entry-point group; everything\n  it runs was installed beside it. Worth shipping rather than snippeting,\n  because a host with plugins compiled into it can always be accused of knowing\n  about them.\n- `make all` (build and install everything), `make cms` (build, install and\n  launch), `make cms-build`, and a rewritten `make help`.\n\nTests: 134 TS, 82 Python. New `test_two_packages_one_point.py` encodes the CMS\nmodel against the runtime rather than the example, so CI runs it without either\npackage installed: two tiers filling the same points, uninstalling one leaving\nthe other whole, and buying the paid tier mid-flight.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* chore: do not commit wheel build artefacts\n\nA failed setuptools wheel build left `examples/cms/cms/build/` behind and it\nwent in with the fix that removed the need for it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: a live CMS demo, with the pip install as the interactive part\n\n/examples/cms/demo runs the CMS example on the documentation site, and the\nbutton on it is the point: it does to an in-browser host exactly what\n`pip install cms-pro` does to a real one — adds a distribution to the\nenvironment. Refresh, and three plugins appear in three contribution points\nthat already existed, with nothing in the application rebuilt or renamed.\n\nThe page explains what has to be true for that to work: one wheel carrying both\nhalves under `share/`, an entry point that makes installing it publishing it,\nfrontend manifests written in Python so the plugin list is complete before any\nJavaScript is fetched, and a server that rescans rather than restarts. And why\nthe paid tier needs no plugin API of its own.\n\nTwo things came out of embedding a Tailwind application in a Docusaurus page,\nand both are in the CSS with the reasons:\n\n- Tailwind''s Preflight is a *global* reset — it would strip the margins and\n  headings off the surrounding documentation. The demo imports the theme and\n  the utilities and not the third part. The music store needs no such care\n  because Primer draws with CSS-in-JS, which is a real difference between design\n  systems and worth the example having found it.\n- A reset written **outside a cascade layer beats every Tailwind utility**.\n  Buttons came out transparent and every border drew nothing until the demo''s\n  scoped reset moved into `@layer base` — which is exactly why Preflight is in\n  a layer.\n\nThe extension modules are the example''s own un-built `index.js` files, bundled\nas source text and imported from a `blob:` URL: a genuine runtime `import()` of\na URL the build did not know, without copying the example into the site.\n\nVerified in the browser: 11 checks, from \"free tier only\" through installing the\npaid package to a Pro lifecycle step running — and the docs page''s own headings\nstill 48px.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* dededupe\n\n* fix(docs): keep the music store''s theme off the pages around it\n\n`DatalayerThemeProvider` writes its theme onto `document.body` as an inline\nstyle — the Primer tokens, and with them `font-family`, `font-size`,\n`background-color`, `color`, `-webkit-font-smoothing` and `text-rendering`. That\nis right for an application that owns the page, and the music store run on its\nown is exactly that: an overlay renders at the document root, outside every\nprovider, and inherits its theme from the body it lands in.\n\nEmbedded in this documentation site it was wrong twice over. Inline styles beat\nevery stylesheet, so the font, background and text colour of the page changed —\nand because Docusaurus is a single-page application, they stayed changed after\nnavigating away from the demo.\n\nThe fix leaves the tokens alone (`--fgColor-…`, `--text-…` collide with nothing\nDocusaurus uses, and the portals genuinely need them) and removes the six\ndeclarations that are appearance rather than tokens. An observer rather than a\none-off, because the provider rewrites them whenever the theme is recomputed —\nincluding on hover, when the header''s overlay mounts a provider of its own.\n\nThe alternative was to stop using `ThemedProvider` in the demo, which would have\nmeant the demo no longer running the code the example runs. Better to keep the\nexample honest and pay for the embedding on the embedding side.\n\nMeasured before and after, on a plain page, the demo page, and a plain page\nreached by client-side navigation from the demo: body font, background and\ncolour are now identical in all three. Both overlay paths — the cart summary and\na plugin row in the manager — open with the body still untouched.\n\nAlso isolates `test_find_ui_looks_where_a_wheel_puts_it` from the real\n`sys.prefix`. It began failing once `datalayer_music_example` was installed,\nwhich put `share/…/apps/music` there — the same class of environment leak\nalready fixed for the entry-point group.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: examples\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlZHtCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAbCEQAINUNvyDwrVKB4U2gY7dQoUF\nhDlstBBgJodubCkxobFZ7xAFAlCMfqP8oJfvHnBl6qbbjGzZgX/qRb0JIwJt6zm8\nGQCJfjmeKF0h+eqk1ZZqI40H4U1g9MZsR5pH/RT8KM3iocRv+m6JYP/bI80QcPQB\nC427Min6DkJV/GVmOwhYlgpEw/7VF7ZKHxyzUko1x4hW8nYm/c8e15p/izArVxBb\nZB4F9Fb9RVR4ixUqozWQeFnd3bUvtPjpaGQr3jVjsU9CGq0NswsSZkcMlZCT8/m5\nSKXHJ8zNA/8UnzGrAJI7MY5SQqwBopJIlk7gbjds9uRjd1+x6z0TzlKXye9/HGDw\n/2W+mbHLRd2UQjwb7YxX12FCD0AT97csi6Sxg6Wxj8TMEmA/1ti9nWBZNzloBX+L\nuWx4D9WyEzwQDmCF/QkAdeZCYxTANUDVSPlBoU1rMroFy3DLz4HC9nVFaI8KwLQO\nc6lk65glziGsui9JzmSZRjcAh+kkewagukmxYdvEczRVadLC+LFW+155LUTu1ynu\n+zTU+EYMi3gpVadG4/LE6ao80GqbAjG+bQ1jORtm9Q6id8Y+2HwXaBPFoi8nEAvK\nUUr1fAQJ6dfoWqd1nxa2EP1L0lO5gdEoNJPKQ2gaNYz+AaMDIsPmFw6AFM5Lpyah\nMgAqp3N/BwSkqw6koSka\n=Dd/c\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-31T14:38:37Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/datalayer/reactor/commit/d2b78f48e4832d915928925d86e14453a99ee445","node_id":"C_kwDOR_wP0doAKGQyYjc4ZjQ4ZTQ4MzJkOTE1OTI4OTI1ZDg2ZTE0NDUzYTk5ZWU0NDU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/datalayer/reactor/commit/5d785e1fede8569836dd4fa99751a2ff57bb51a3","sha":"5d785e1fede8569836dd4fa99751a2ff57bb51a3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/datalayer/reactor/commits/5d785e1fede8569836dd4fa99751a2ff57bb51a3"}],"sha":"d2b78f48e4832d915928925d86e14453a99ee445","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/datalayer/reactor/commits/d2b78f48e4832d915928925d86e14453a99ee445"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/226720?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/echarles","id":226720,"login":"echarles","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjIyNjcyMA==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/echarles","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/datalayer/reactor/commits/5d785e1fede8569836dd4fa99751a2ff57bb51a3/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-31T10:04:48Z","email":"226720+echarles@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Eric Charles"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-31T10:04:48Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: extension points (#8)\n\n* extension points\n\n* docs\n\n* lint\n\n* contributions\n\n* music: plugin\n\n* Plugin metadata, cross-tier declarations, lazy activation and the plugin graph\n\nFour features on the injection system, plus a reusable plugin built on them.\n\nPresentation: `displayName`, `description`, `octicon` and `emoji` on both\n`ReactorExtension` and `PluginManifest`, read back through `getMetadata` /\n`useExtensionsMetadata` and `GET /plugins`. The icon is named by id rather\nthan imported, so a Python manifest can name the same icon as a TypeScript\none and the host decides what it draws.\n\nCross-tier declarations, in both directions and both strengths:\n`optionalBackendPlugins` joins `requiredBackendPlugins` (required gates\nrendering, optional never does — `useBackendPlugin` is how an extension asks),\nand `frontend_dependencies` / `optional_frontend_dependencies` do the mirror\non the manifest. The latter are declared rather than enforced: the platform\ncannot see a browser, so `frontend_requirements(active)` and\n`GET /plugins/frontend-requirements` answer for a caller that can.\n\nLazy activation: `defineLazyExtension` fetches a module after `start()`\nreturns. Modules are fetched in parallel and activated in dependency order,\neach waking subscribers, so nothing on screen waits for a plugin that renders\nnothing. Metadata is complete before a module lands, so a plugin list is whole\nfrom the first frame. Overlapping passes — StrictMode''s start/stop/start —\nshare one fetch and activate once.\n\nThe graph: `describePluginGraph` derives nodes and edges from what plugins\nalready declare, so there is nothing to keep up to date. It stops at data —\nno geometry, colour or component — which is what keeps a charting library out\nof the framework. Extensions and manifests can now declare the points they\n*offer*, since the registry only ever knows contributors.\n\n`plugins/@datalayer/reactor-graph` draws it with echarts, promoted out of the\nexample so other applications can install it: no example imports, and the\nbackend''s address and plugin list arrive as props. `plugins/README.md` says\nwhat belongs in that folder and why. `npm run build` and `make publish-npm`\nnow cover the reactor and every plugin, discovered rather than listed.\n\nTests: 45 TypeScript, 27 Python.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* plugins\n\n* lint\n\n* music\n\n* grpaph\n\n* versions\n\n* manager\n\n* fix: ci\n\n* fix: ci\n\n* readme\n\n* test\n\n* readme\n\n* docs\n\n* Automatic application of license header\n\n* readme\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: github-actions[bot] <github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>","tree":{"sha":"e938ff3aa52b27f9d5c08e55879314aef75e53c6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/datalayer/reactor/git/trees/e938ff3aa52b27f9d5c08e55879314aef75e53c6"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/datalayer/reactor/git/commits/5d785e1fede8569836dd4fa99751a2ff57bb51a3","verification":{"payload":"tree e938ff3aa52b27f9d5c08e55879314aef75e53c6\nparent d78df4b85af0893d1bc4ee9790715e48bfbd71bc\nauthor Eric Charles <226720+echarles@users.noreply.github.com> 1788170688 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788170688 +0200\n\nfeat: extension points (#8)\n\n* extension points\n\n* docs\n\n* lint\n\n* contributions\n\n* music: plugin\n\n* Plugin metadata, cross-tier declarations, lazy activation and the plugin graph\n\nFour features on the injection system, plus a reusable plugin built on them.\n\nPresentation: `displayName`, `description`, `octicon` and `emoji` on both\n`ReactorExtension` and `PluginManifest`, read back through `getMetadata` /\n`useExtensionsMetadata` and `GET /plugins`. The icon is named by id rather\nthan imported, so a Python manifest can name the same icon as a TypeScript\none and the host decides what it draws.\n\nCross-tier declarations, in both directions and both strengths:\n`optionalBackendPlugins` joins `requiredBackendPlugins` (required gates\nrendering, optional never does — `useBackendPlugin` is how an extension asks),\nand `frontend_dependencies` / `optional_frontend_dependencies` do the mirror\non the manifest. The latter are declared rather than enforced: the platform\ncannot see a browser, so `frontend_requirements(active)` and\n`GET /plugins/frontend-requirements` answer for a caller that can.\n\nLazy activation: `defineLazyExtension` fetches a module after `start()`\nreturns. Modules are fetched in parallel and activated in dependency order,\neach waking subscribers, so nothing on screen waits for a plugin that renders\nnothing. Metadata is complete before a module lands, so a plugin list is whole\nfrom the first frame. Overlapping passes — StrictMode''s start/stop/start —\nshare one fetch and activate once.\n\nThe graph: `describePluginGraph` derives nodes and edges from what plugins\nalready declare, so there is nothing to keep up to date. It stops at data —\nno geometry, colour or component — which is what keeps a charting library out\nof the framework. Extensions and manifests can now declare the points they\n*offer*, since the registry only ever knows contributors.\n\n`plugins/@datalayer/reactor-graph` draws it with echarts, promoted out of the\nexample so other applications can install it: no example imports, and the\nbackend''s address and plugin list arrive as props. `plugins/README.md` says\nwhat belongs in that folder and why. `npm run build` and `make publish-npm`\nnow cover the reactor and every plugin, discovered rather than listed.\n\nTests: 45 TypeScript, 27 Python.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* plugins\n\n* lint\n\n* music\n\n* grpaph\n\n* versions\n\n* manager\n\n* fix: ci\n\n* fix: ci\n\n* readme\n\n* test\n\n* readme\n\n* docs\n\n* Automatic application of license header\n\n* readme\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: github-actions[bot] <github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlVHACRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAR4gQAC0VHewPG9I7MGh4F5JfIq9r\nDniTkyYRmCH8WVV7z3oAl38xz5f99tyiWRmLbumn5ZECiVimwbQID7a68gs0DIz2\nRCvXK/WsawQuzQn53fAsPKir3cnzdhyh9UzaIW0sE6l3VAraqyoGM3iJ4g+IIi1P\nMr8iRXrdbkaKM+0daosXKtg06rcAYzrJUwZB7PUEKF40Itg+jZ7Xz0m5AjFTkor1\nDOiVsWHRjB7xt47SPRzvraO0veIw+OLmi4rF1yvgdvWAtARV3r8fwgSP6CBuEMkg\nzVAwSCXLXDvdUrsiZCtigepox3I8opjjAPj2S0P0yfa54POas2+clgx/urxDNWKd\nXYxTlyNMszxaD47zDCFYj1vaGAIOKp0nTRmP7FJnxkpY8fpWgPlCImFLkp9lbicu\nvqvcAhKjp06i4c7A+VCvVpZuKBTp3+/GMmDEkWjeUXyyiGTwjgGYxv5/NKOa2Hif\n37I8n2R9ICnnniTKpy0n00B+vpOesqWoYhbn/nxNL25xDHOb5WuFi5RPjG+TeTIo\n/Q7tp3gGFHlQZmFJVBu9XUKjVug6nCoNVJtuAsdiZnLEmRkIaLR4Ddi6z3gkUyJP\nCFy4eLlcgwt5fkmVIFYc9UXjsCmkH/LG+6jMN05vTjGaGG9DTQ1zZKz7uXju8Bjo\nRW+TB833EnZann769DU5\n=86tU\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-31T10:04:48Z"}},"committer":{"ava